# The record track control block, and the same missing class as
# pc_mediaswcodec.te.
#
# Video recording reached the audio stage and stopped there:
#
#     E AudioRecord: createRecord_l(0): AudioFlinger could not create record
#                    track, status: -2147483646
#     E StagefrightRecorder: audio source is not initialized
#     E MediaRecorder: start failed: -2147483648
#
# The kernel says what actually failed, and it is not audio:
#
#     binder: 1459:1403 translate fd failed
#     binder: 1403:1459 transaction reply to 1569:3625 failed
#
# 1403 is audioserver, 1569 mediaserver. audioserver opened the input stream
# successfully, built the record track, and could not hand the shared control
# block back: binder_translate_fd asks SELinux whether the receiving domain may
# use the file, and the answer was no. mediaserver never receives a control
# block, AudioSource fails to construct, and StagefrightRecorder reports the
# audio source as uninitialised. Nothing in the audio path is at fault.
#
# AOSP grants precisely this in private/mediaserver.te:
#     allow mediaserver audioserver:memfd_file { getattr map read write };
# and, as in pc_mediaswcodec.te, memfd_file is an Android common kernel class
# that mainline does not implement, so the object is a tmpfs file carrying
# audioserver's tmpfs type and that rule cannot match.
#
# The fd half of the transfer is already permitted: mediaserver.te has
# binder_call(mediaserver, binderservicedomain) and audioserver is one, which
# grants audioserver:fd use. The denial is on class file, not fd, which is
# consistent with that. Only the file half is missing.
#
# No MLS attribute is needed. Both domains run at s0, so l1 eq l2 satisfies the
# write constraint on its own, and pc_audioserver.te has already marked
# audioserver_tmpfs mlstrustedobject for the app case.
allow mediaserver audioserver_tmpfs:file { getattr read write map };

# The client end of the Codec2 FMQ. Granting mediaswcodec the queue in
# pc_mediaswcodec.te only covered the service side; mediaserver holds the other
# end of the same memfd and was refused on it:
#
#     avc: denied { read write } comm="oid.aac.encoder"
#          path="/memfd:MessageQueue (deleted)" scontext=u:r:mediaserver:s0
#          tcontext=u:object_r:tmpfs:s0 tclass=file
#
# The consequence is not a stray audit line. The BufferPool cannot hand buffers
# across, so registration fails outright and every buffer is rejected:
#
#     E libbinder.IPCThreadState: Binder transaction failure. BR_FAILED_REPLY,
#                                 error: -1 (Operation not permitted)
#     W Codec2-AIDL-BufferTypes: registerSender -- returned error: 4.
#     E Codec2-AIDL-BufferTypes: Failed to send buffer with BufferPool.
#     E Codec2-AIDL-BufferTypes: Invalid C2Buffer::data.
#
# which strands the recording pipeline one step further back, in the camera:
#
#     W CameraSource: Waiting on an available memory base timed out.
#                     Dropping a recording frame.
#     E MPEG4Writer: The number of recorded samples is 0
#
# CameraSource hands its metadata buffers to the encoder and gets none back,
# because the encoder never receives them, so its pool drains and every
# subsequent frame is dropped on a 200ms timeout. A recording that runs for six
# seconds and saves nothing is what that looks like from the UI.
#
# Same shape as the camera queue, which needed exactly this pair: the HAL end
# was granted first and the denial moved to cameraserver, which is why
# pc_cameraserver.te carries
#     allow cameraserver tmpfs:file { read write map };
# An FMQ has two ends and both of them need the rule.
allow mediaserver tmpfs:file { getattr read write map };

# The app end of the thumbnail buffer. Recording is fixed and the files are
# valid; what is missing is the ability to see one.
#
#     avc: denied { read write } path="/memfd:MetadataRetrieverClient (deleted)"
#          scontext=u:r:untrusted_app:s0:c72,c256,c512,c768
#          tcontext=u:object_r:mediaserver_tmpfs:s0 tclass=file
#
# MediaMetadataRetriever decodes a frame in mediaserver and returns it to the
# client through IMemory, which is a memfd on this kernel. The app cannot map
# it, so the transfer fails and the filmstrip has nothing to draw:
#
#     E libbinder.IMemory: transaction failed fd=-2147483647, size=0,
#                          err=-2147483646 (FAILED_TRANSACTION)
#     E MediaMetadataRetrieverJNI: getEmbeddedPicture: Call failed.
#     E MediaMetadataRetrieverJNI: getFrameAtTime: videoFrame is a NULL pointer
#
# The recording itself completed: both tracks stopped with status 0, the file
# was published out of .pending, and it is a structurally valid MP4 with a
# 13MB mdat. Only the thumbnail is missing, which from the filmstrip is
# indistinguishable from the recording not having been saved.
#
# private/app.te grants appdomain the equivalent for its neighbours,
#     allow appdomain audioserver_tmpfs:file { getattr map read write };
#     allow appdomain system_server_tmpfs:file { getattr map read write };
# and simply omits mediaserver_tmpfs. cameraserver_tmpfs was missing from that
# same list, which is why pc_cameraserver.te already carries this exact pair.
allow appdomain mediaserver_tmpfs:file { getattr map read write };

# And the MLS half, for the same reason it was needed for the camera queue. The
# app is s0:c72,... and the memfd is plain s0. Reading needs the source to
# dominate the target, which a categorised app does, so read alone would pass;
# writing needs equivalence, and s0 does not equal a categorised level, so the
# mapping is still refused. IMemory maps the region shared, so both are asked
# for and an allow rule cannot lift an MLS constraint.
#
# Added from private policy because the declaration in public/mediaserver.te
# cannot be edited, the way AOSP does it for traced_tmpfs and heapprofd_tmpfs.
typeattribute mediaserver_tmpfs mlstrustedobject;

# The buffers FrameDecoder actually reads, and the crash they caused.
#
#     avc: denied { read write } scontext=u:r:mediaserver:s0
#          tcontext=u:object_r:mediaextractor_tmpfs:s0 tclass=file
#
# mediaserver did not merely fail to produce a thumbnail, it aborted:
#
#     Abort message: MediaBuffer.cpp:145 CHECK(offset + length <= mSize) failed
#         #05 MediaBuffer::set_range
#         #06 BpMediaSource::readMultiple
#         #08 FrameDecoder::extractInternalUsingBlockModel
#         #09 FrameDecoder::extractFrame
#
# readMultiple carries sample data in shared memory owned by mediaextractor.
# Unable to map it, mediaserver ends up with a zero sized buffer and then sets a
# real range on it, and the CHECK is fatal rather than diagnostic. Two
# tombstones this boot, both mediaserver, both here. Fixing the mapping removes
# the crash as well as the missing thumbnail.
#
# private/mediaserver.te:10 already grants exactly this as
#     allow mediaserver mediaextractor:memfd_file { getattr map read write };
allow mediaserver mediaextractor_tmpfs:file { getattr map read write };
